#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Year 4 Creative Arts Written Core batch 01: painting colour/mood through melody w01-l1–w06-l2 (12)."""
from arts_support import L, pv, create, VIS, MUS

LESSONS = [
L('s2-arts-y4-w01-l1', VIS, ['mood', 'warm colour', 'cool colour', 'colour choice'],
  "Colour choices change the mood of a painting. Warm colours such as red, orange and yellow often feel energetic, sunny or fiery. Cool colours such as blue, green and purple often feel calm, quiet or chilly. Artists choose a main colour family on purpose, then add a small contrast so the eye has somewhere to rest. When you look at a painting, name the main colour family, say what mood it suggests and point to one detail that supports that feeling.",
  "Example: a harbour scene uses deep blues and soft greens for water and sky, with one small orange boat. The cool colours make the scene feel quiet, and the warm boat becomes the focal point. Viewers notice the boat first because warm colours stand out against cool ones.",
  "A painting of a playground uses mostly reds and yellows with one blue shadow under the slide. Name the main colour family, the mood it suggests and what the blue shadow does.",
  "Strong answer: 'Warm colours dominate, so it feels lively and energetic. The cool blue shadow cools one corner and shows where the light is blocked.' The answer links colour family to mood and explains the contrast.",
  "Thinking that warm colours are always happy and cool colours are always sad. Mood depends on how colours are used together, how light or dark they are and what the subject is.",
  "Find or imagine two paintings: one warm-dominated and one cool-dominated. Write three sentences for each naming the colour family, the mood and one supporting detail. Then sketch a small scene twice, once in warm colours and once in cool colours, and label the mood of each."),
L('s2-arts-y4-w01-l2', VIS, ['warm palette', 'cool palette', 'brush stroke', 'mixing on a palette'],
  "Painting with warm and cool colours means planning a palette before you start. Mix warm colours on one side of the palette and cool colours on the other so they do not muddy each other. Lay large areas with flat strokes first, then add details with the tip of the brush. Rinse between warm and cool mixes. Cover the table, wear a smock and wash brushes carefully. Light areas first, darker accents last.",
  "Example: Jin paints a sunset beach. She mixes orange and pink for the sky (warm) and blue-green for the sea (cool). Wide flat strokes fill the sky, then a thin cool tip stroke marks the horizon. She keeps the mixes separate so the sunset stays bright.",
  "You are painting a garden at dawn with a warm pink sky and cool green plants. Plan which colours to mix, which strokes to use and the painting order.",
  "Strong answer: 'Mix pink and pale orange for sky; mix two greens for plants. Sky with wide flat strokes first, then plant tip strokes, then dark accents.' Order and separate mixes keep colours clear.",
  "Mixing warm and cool paints in the same puddle without planning. That often makes dull brown, so keep families apart until you choose a controlled mix.",
  "Paint or crayon a scene that uses a warm area and a cool area. On the back write your palette recipes and which stroke made which part. If you have no paint, use coloured pencils in warm and cool groups."),
L('s2-arts-y4-w02-l1', VIS, ['tint', 'tone', 'shade', 'value'],
  "A tint is a colour lightened with white. A shade is a colour darkened with a small amount of black or a darker colour. A tone is a colour softened with grey or a little of its opposite. Value means how light or dark a colour is. Practise a value scale of one hue from pale tint to deep shade so you can choose the right strength for sky, midground and shadow.",
  "Example: Ana makes five greens: pale tint with white, mid green, green mixed with a touch of grey (tone), dark green with more blue, and a shade with a tiny dab of black. She uses the tint for sunlit leaves and the shade for under the tree.",
  "You have blue paint, white, black and grey. How would you make a pale sky tint, a mid-blue sea and a dark shade under a boat?",
  "Strong answer: 'Sky: blue + white tint. Sea: mostly blue. Under the boat: blue + tiny black (or more dark blue) for shade.' Small amounts of dark colour prevent muddy mixes.",
  "Adding large amounts of black to darken a colour. Black quickly dulls mixes, so add tiny amounts or use a darker related colour first.",
  "Make a five-step value scale for one colour. Then paint or colour a simple object using at least one tint, one mid value and one shade. Label each."),
L('s2-arts-y4-w02-l2', VIS, ['landscape', 'horizon', 'aerial perspective', 'layered depth'],
  "A landscape painting shows land or sea and sky. The horizon is where sky meets land or water. Things closer are often larger, more detailed and stronger in colour; things farther away can be smaller, lighter and cooler (aerial perspective). Paint the sky and distant layers first, then the midground, then the foreground details.",
  "Example: Bo paints hills. Distant hills are pale blue-green with soft edges. Mid hills are stronger green. A dark tree in the foreground is large and detailed. The horizon sits about one-third down so the sky has room.",
  "Plan a landscape of a river valley. Say where the horizon goes and how you will show near and far using size and colour.",
  "Strong answer: 'Horizon one-third down. Far hills small and pale cool green; near trees larger and darker with more detail.' Size and value create depth.",
  "Putting every detail at the same strength. Without lighter distant layers, the landscape looks flat.",
  "Paint or draw a landscape with a clear horizon, one distant layer, one midground and one foreground detail. Label which parts are tinted or shaded to show depth."),
L('s2-arts-y4-w03-l1', VIS, ['composition', 'foreground', 'background', 'focal point'],
  "Composition is how parts of a picture are arranged. The foreground is the area that looks nearest, the background is farthest and the midground sits between them. A focal point is the place you want viewers to notice first. Artists guide the eye with size, contrast, placement and leading lines. Avoid placing the main subject dead centre every time; slightly off-centre often feels more interesting.",
  "Example: a painting of a lighthouse puts a large lighthouse in the left foreground, rocks in the midground and a pale sea in the background. A path of light leads the eye to the lighthouse, so it is the focal point.",
  "A picture of a soccer game has a tiny ball in the far corner and huge empty grass in front. Suggest two composition changes to make the ball the focal point.",
  "Strong answer: 'Make the ball larger in the foreground or midground, and use a player’s gaze or a line of shadow leading to it.' Size and leading lines strengthen the focal point.",
  "Thinking composition means filling every space. Empty space (negative space) can help the focal point stand out.",
  "Cut three paper shapes (near, mid, far) and arrange them two ways: weak composition and strong composition. Photograph or sketch both and write why the strong one works better."),
L('s2-arts-y4-w03-l2', VIS, ['thumbnail', 'rule of thirds', 'balance', 'planning sketch'],
  "Before a final artwork, artists plan with thumbnails: small quick sketches that test composition. Mentally divide the page into thirds both ways; placing a focal point near an intersection often feels balanced. Check that large shapes balance smaller ones and that the eye has a path through the picture. Choose the best thumbnail before you start the final piece.",
  "Example: Mia draws three thumbnails of a market stall. Thumbnail 2 places the fruit stall on a left third-line with a path leading in from the right. She enlarges that plan for her final painting.",
  "You want to paint a tree and a house. Sketch two thumbnail ideas in words: one with the tree centred, one using thirds. Say which you would choose and why.",
  "Strong answer: 'Centred tree feels static. Tree on left third with house on right third and a path between feels balanced and leads the eye.' Planning explains the choice.",
  "Skipping thumbnails and starting large immediately. Planning saves time and prevents crowded or empty compositions.",
  "Draw four thumbnails for one subject. Mark thirds lightly, circle your focal point on the best one and write two reasons you chose it. Then begin a larger draft from that plan."),
L('s2-arts-y4-w04-l1', VIS, ['viewpoint', 'meaning', 'evidence from the artwork', 'artist intention'],
  "Looking carefully at an artwork means describing what you see, then interpreting possible meaning with evidence from the work. Viewpoint can mean where the artist placed the viewer (looking up, looking down, close-up) and also your own opinion. You can say what you think the work is about if you point to colours, composition, subject and mood. Different viewers may disagree politely when they use evidence.",
  "Example: In a close-up of worn boots on a doorstep, Sam says, 'I think the painting is about hard work because the boots are large in the foreground, dusty brown and placed at the entrance like someone has just come home.' He used size, colour and placement as evidence.",
  "A painting shows a tiny figure under a huge stormy sky. Suggest one meaning and give two pieces of evidence from the artwork.",
  "Strong answer: 'It may show feeling small in nature because the figure is tiny compared with the sky, and the cool dark colours feel overwhelming.' Meaning is linked to scale and colour.",
  "Stating a meaning with no link to what is actually in the picture. Opinions need visual evidence.",
  "Choose a published artwork (book or approved gallery image). Write four sentences: subject, viewpoint, possible meaning and two evidence details. Stay polite about other opinions."),
L('s2-arts-y4-w04-l2', VIS, ['response', 'compare', 'personal connection', 'respectful language'],
  "A written or spoken response to a painting uses describe → respond → connect. Describe the subject and elements, respond with feeling or idea plus reason, then connect to your own experience or another artwork without claiming your view is the only right one. Use respectful language about artists and other viewers.",
  "Example: Cleo writes, 'I see layered cool blues with one warm window light. It feels hopeful because the light is the brightest spot. It reminds me of waiting for family to come home. Someone else might feel lonely instead, and that is also fair.'",
  "Respond to an imagined painting of a crowded train in warm oranges. Write describe, respond and connect sentences.",
  "Strong answer: 'Describe: crowded carriages in orange light. Respond: busy and tense because warm colours are packed tightly. Connect: like my peak-hour bus.' Structure is complete and polite.",
  "Only saying 'I like it' or 'I hate it'. A Year 4 response needs reasons from the artwork.",
  "Write a full response (describe, respond, connect) to a painting you choose. Share with a family member and note one different opinion they had and the evidence they used."),
L('s2-arts-y4-w05-l1', VIS, ['painting project', 'design brief', 'criteria', 'process diary'],
  "A painting project needs a design brief: subject, mood, colour plan, size and success criteria. Work in stages—thumbnail, underpainting or light sketch, main colour areas, then details and reflection. Keep a short process diary of decisions and changes. Protect surfaces and dry layers before adding sharp edges.",
  "Example: Dev’s brief: 'Harbour at dusk, calm mood, cool blues with one warm light, A3 paper.' Criteria: clear foreground boat, aerial perspective in hills, tidy edges. His diary notes: 'Moved boat left for better balance.'",
  "Write a design brief for a painting of your street at night, including mood, palette and two success criteria.",
  "Strong answer: 'Subject: my street at night. Mood: quiet. Palette: cool blues/violets with warm window yellows. Criteria: clear focal light and layered depth.' Brief guides the work.",
  "Judging the project only by whether it looks photographic. Criteria should match the skills taught (mood, composition, value).",
  "Complete a painting project from brief to finished piece. Keep a three-entry process diary and a final reflection against your criteria."),
L('s2-arts-y4-w05-l2', VIS, ['showcase', 'artist statement', 'two stars and a wish', 'unit reflection'],
  "At the end of a painting unit, select work for a showcase, write an artist statement and invite kind feedback. An artist statement names the title, intention, key colour/composition choices and one challenge you solved. Visitors give two stars and a wish with specific evidence. Reflect on what you learned about mood, value and composition.",
  "Example: Lena’s statement: 'Quiet Jetty. I used cool tints for distance and a warm lamp as focal point. Challenge: muddy water—I remixed on the palette.' A visitor stars the depth and the lamp, and wishes for a clearer horizon line.",
  "Write an artist statement for a landscape you made and one wish you would give another student’s work kindly.",
  "Strong answer: Statement names intention and one technique; wish is specific and kind, such as 'add a lighter distant hill for depth.'",
  "Displaying work without labels or reflection. Statements help viewers and help you notice your growth.",
  "Mount or photograph your best painting with a label and statement. Collect two stars and a wish from someone at home and write a five-sentence unit reflection."),
L('s2-arts-y4-w06-l1', MUS, ['melody', 'pitch', 'step', 'leap'],
  "A melody is a tune made of pitches that move by steps (to a nearby note) or leaps (skipping notes). Higher pitches sound higher; lower pitches sound lower. Melodies often have a clear starting note, a shape that rises and falls and a finishing note that feels settled. Clap the rhythm first, then sing or play the pitches carefully.",
  "Example: the start of a familiar playground tune moves mostly by steps, then one leap up before falling back. Students map the shape with a finger in the air: up, up, down, settle.",
  "A four-note melody goes C–D–E–C. Label each move as step or leap and describe the overall shape.",
  "Strong answer: 'C to D step, D to E step, E to C leap down. Shape rises then returns home.'",
  "Thinking louder equals higher pitch. Loudness is dynamics; pitch is how high or low the note is.",
  "Invent a six-note melody using at least four steps and one leap. Draw its contour, sing or hum it and teach it to a family member."),
L('s2-arts-y4-w06-l2', MUS, ['phrase', 'breath mark', 'practice tempo', 'melody practice'],
  "Playing or singing a simple melody well means dividing it into phrases (musical sentences), breathing or resetting at phrase ends and practising slowly before performance tempo. Watch posture, listen for accurate pitch and keep a steady beat. Mark tricky leaps and isolate them.",
  "Example: Kai practises a recorder melody at a slow tempo, breathes every four beats and repeats the leap from G to C until it is clean, then speeds up gradually.",
  "A student rushes the second phrase and misses a leap. Write two practice fixes.",
  "Strong answer: 'Slow the tempo and clap the rhythm first; practise only the leap five times, then join phrases.' Isolation plus slow practice works.",
  "Repeating the whole melody fast when one bar is wrong. Fix the bar, then reconnect.",
  "Choose or invent a short melody. Mark phrase breaths, practise slowly, then perform for someone and note one improvement."),
]

